export function ownership(add,cpp){
add('lifetimes-views','observer-owner','An observer does not own the object',
'A raw pointer can express observation while another object owns the resource. The observer is usable only while the owner keeps the object alive. Destroying or resetting the owner invalidates the observer even though the pointer variable still contains an address.\n\nKeep ownership visible in the surrounding interface. This example stops using the observer before resetting its unique owner.',
cpp('iostream memory',"    auto owner = std::make_unique<int>(42);\n    int *observer = owner.get();\n    std::cout << *observer << \"\\n\";\n    owner.reset();\n    observer = nullptr;\n    std::cout << \"empty=\" << !owner << \"\\n\";"),'42\nempty=1',
'get produces a borrowed pointer. reset destroys the int; assigning nullptr to the observer records that it must no longer be used.',
'A non-null pointer is not proof that its target is still alive.',
['Does get transfer ownership?','No, it returns an observer','Yes, the caller must delete it','Only for int objects'],'The unique_ptr remains the sole owner.',
'Write a function accepting const int* and document whether null is permitted. Compare that observer interface with accepting unique_ptr<int>.');
add('lifetimes-views','temporary-lifetime','A named owner can outlive an expression',
'Temporary objects normally last to the end of their full expression. Borrowing from a temporary and retaining that borrow can therefore leave a dangling view immediately after the statement.\n\nStore an owning result first when later operations need its storage. Binding some references can extend a temporary’s lifetime, but lifetime extension has specific rules and is not propagated through arbitrary returned views.',
cpp('iostream string string_view',"    std::string owner = make_name();\n    std::string_view view = owner;\n    std::cout << view << \"\\n\";","std::string make_name() {\n    return \"Ada Lovelace\";\n}"),'Ada Lovelace',
'The returned string becomes the named owner. The view is used while that owner remains in scope.',
'string_view view=make_name() would retain a borrow after the returned temporary string is destroyed.',
['Why is this view valid at the output?','The named string owner is still alive','string_view copies all characters','Every returned temporary lasts until main ends'],'The owner’s scope covers the use of its borrowed storage.',
'Draw lifetimes for the safe code and the dangling alternative without executing undefined behavior. Rewrite an API to return an owning string.');
add('lifetimes-views','vector-invalidation','Reallocation invalidates element observers',
'A vector owns a contiguous allocation. When growth requires a new allocation, its elements move or copy and old element references, pointers, and iterators become invalid.\n\nAn index can be reacquired after growth when the operation preserves the logical ordering you rely on. reserve can reduce reallocations, but it is not a general lifetime proof for every future mutation.',
cpp('iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values{10, 20};\n    std::size_t chosen = 1;\n    values.reserve(100);\n    values.push_back(30);\n    std::cout << values.at(chosen) << \" size=\" << values.size() << \"\\n\";"),'20 size=3',
'The code keeps an index and performs a fresh checked access after potential reallocation. Appending preserves the earlier element order.',
'An index can name a different record after insertion or erasure before it. Stable numeric position and stable identity are different contracts.',
['What is safely reacquired here after growth?','The element through a still-valid index','A previously dangling pointer','The old allocation address'],'The access derives a new reference from the current vector.',
'Insert at the beginning and observe how the same index changes meaning. Design a record lookup around stable IDs instead.');
add('lifetimes-views','view-mutation','A view can expose mutations without owning them',
'A mutable span is a borrowed window into existing elements. Writing through it changes the owner’s elements. A span<const T> instead offers read-only access through that view while still depending on the owner’s lifetime.\n\nNeither span variant automatically synchronizes concurrent access. Const access through one path does not make mutation through another path impossible.',
cpp('array iostream span',"    std::array<int, 3> values{1, 2, 3};\n    std::span<int> middle(values.data() + 1, 1);\n    middle.front() = 9;\n    std::span<const int> read_only(values);\n    for (int value : read_only)\n        std::cout << value;\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";"),'193',
'The mutable view aliases the second element. The read-only view observes the already changed owner.',
'Changing an owner can invalidate views even when the view itself is const.',
['Does span<const int> keep the array alive?','No','Yes','Only when constructed from std::array'],'A span packages access; ownership stays with the array.',
'Pass a span<const int> to a sum function accepting arrays and vectors. Document which caller mutations would invalidate the borrow.');
add('lifetimes-views','shared-weak','A weak observer can detect expired shared ownership',
'shared_ptr represents shared lifetime ownership, which should be chosen only when the design needs several independent owners. weak_ptr observes the shared ownership state without extending the object’s lifetime.\n\nlock either obtains a temporary shared owner or reports expiration. This avoids the unsafe pattern of checking a raw pointer and then assuming the target remains alive.',
cpp('iostream memory',"    std::weak_ptr<int> observer;\n    {\n        auto owner = std::make_shared<int>(7);\n        observer = owner;\n        if (auto held = observer.lock())\n            std::cout << *held << \"\\n\";\n    }\n    std::cout << \"expired=\" << observer.expired() << \"\\n\";"),'7\nexpired=1',
'The locked handle temporarily shares ownership inside the scope. After both owning handles are gone, the weak observer reports expiration.',
'Cycles of shared_ptr owners can retain objects indefinitely. A weak relationship can represent a non-owning back edge.',
['Does weak_ptr extend the object lifetime?','No','Yes, until reset','Only after expiration'],'Only successful shared ownership keeps the object alive.',
'Model parent/child ownership and choose which back-reference should be weak. Check expiration after releasing the root owner.');

add('copy-move','rule-zero','Let owning members implement resource management',
'The rule of zero recommends delegating resource management to member types so your class needs no custom destructor, copy constructor, or move constructor. Standard containers already know how to release and copy their owned elements.\n\nThis keeps the public value semantics easier to inspect and reduces the number of operations that must maintain the class invariant.',
cpp('iostream string vector',"    Report first{{\"Ada\", \"Grace\"}};\n    Report copy = first;\n    copy.names[0] = \"Changed\";\n    std::cout << first.names[0] << \" \" << copy.names[0] << \"\\n\";","struct Report {\n    std::vector<std::string> names;\n};"),'Ada Changed',
'Copying Report copies its vector and strings. Mutating the copied name does not change the original report.',
'Adding a manually managed raw owning pointer changes these semantics and can introduce shallow copies and double deletion.',
['Why are the copies independent?','The members have owning value semantics','All structs are automatically deep-copied through pointers','The destructor disables aliasing'],'The standard owning members define the desired copy behavior.',
'Add a second vector member and verify the default operations still satisfy your value contract without custom special members.');
add('copy-move','move-only-types','A move-only member makes ownership transfer explicit',
'A type containing unique_ptr cannot use an automatically generated copy operation that would duplicate that owner. It can often use the generated move operations to transfer its members.\n\nMake a move-only API intentional and test it at compile time. Copyability should reflect the domain contract rather than being discovered accidentally by a caller.',
cpp('iostream memory type_traits utility','    static_assert(!std::is_copy_constructible_v<Page>);\n    static_assert(std::is_move_constructible_v<Page>);\n    Page first{std::make_unique<int>(12)};\n    Page second=std::move(first);\n    std::cout << !first.value << " " << *second.value << "\\n";',"struct Page {\n    std::unique_ptr<int> value;\n};"),'1 12',
'The static assertions describe the generated interface. Moving Page transfers the unique_ptr and leaves the original member empty.',
'A moved-from whole object still needs a documented valid-use contract; do not assume every member retains its previous value.',
['Can this Page be copied by its generated copy constructor?','No','Yes, by duplicating the pointer','Yes, but only once'],'unique_ptr deliberately deletes copying.',
'Add a string label and move the Page again. Check only moved-from properties guaranteed by the relevant member contracts.');
add('copy-move','copy-custom-owner','Define an independent copy when the domain needs one',
'Sometimes an owning representation is move-only but the domain value should be copyable. A custom copy constructor can clone the resource, while copy assignment can prepare a candidate and then commit without throwing.\n\nThis example owns one int to keep the protocol visible. A real buffer owner also needs size, allocation, and element-copy contracts.',
cpp('iostream memory utility',"    Box first{5};\n    Box copy = first;\n    copy.set(9);\n    std::cout << first.get() << \" \" << copy.get() << \"\\n\";","class Box {\n    std::unique_ptr<int> value_;\n\n  public:\n    explicit Box(int value) : value_(std::make_unique<int>(value)) {\n    }\n    Box(const Box &other) : Box(other.get()) {\n    }\n    Box &operator=(const Box &other) {\n        Box candidate(other);\n        value_.swap(candidate.value_);\n        return *this;\n    }\n    int get() const {\n        return *value_;\n    }\n    void set(int value) {\n        *value_ = value;\n    }\n};"),'5 9',
'The copy constructor allocates a different int. The two boxes therefore maintain independent values.',
'Writing only a destructor for an owner can leave generated copying with unsafe shallow ownership behavior.',
['What does the copy constructor duplicate?','The owned value in a separate allocation','Only the raw address','The unique_ptr control block'],'Each successful Box construction owns its own int.',
'Test copy assignment and self-assignment. Add explicitly chosen move operations only after deciding how a moved-from Box may be observed.');
add('copy-move','noexcept-moves','Nonthrowing movement affects container choices',
'A container may choose copying rather than a potentially throwing move when preserving its exception guarantee during reallocation. Declaring a move noexcept is a promise that the operation cannot propagate an exception.\n\nCheck the properties of member operations instead of adding noexcept to silence a diagnostic. A false promise can turn an ordinary exception into program termination.',
cpp('iostream memory type_traits',"    std::cout << std::is_nothrow_move_constructible_v<std::unique_ptr<int>> << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << std::is_copy_constructible_v<std::unique_ptr<int>> << \"\\n\";"),'1\n0',
'unique_ptr’s ownership transfer is nonthrowing and copying is disabled. Those properties make moving it into a vector straightforward.',
'noexcept does not catch exceptions or roll back state; violating it terminates the program.',
['What happens if an exception escapes a noexcept function?','The program terminates','The exception is ignored','The caller receives an optional error'],'noexcept is an enforceable exception-propagation promise.',
'Inspect is_nothrow_move_constructible for an aggregate of standard owning members. Explain which member determines the result.');
add('copy-move','return-elision','Return values without forcing a move',
'Returning a value often permits construction directly in the caller’s result object. C++17 and later guarantee elision for certain prvalue return expressions. Named return optimization has different rules and is not universally guaranteed.\n\nDo not scatter std::move into return statements reflexively: it can interfere with useful elision or obscure the intended ownership transfer.',
cpp('iostream string',"    Message result = make_message();\n    std::cout << result.text << \"\\n\";","struct Message {\n    std::string text;\n};\nMessage make_message() {\n    return Message{\"ready\"};\n}"),'ready',
'The function returns a prvalue Message. The result is constructed without requiring an intermediate copy of Message.',
'A benchmark of one compiler’s named-return optimization is not a universal language guarantee.',
['Does this prvalue return require an intermediate copy?','No','Yes, exactly one','Only when strings are short'],'The prvalue construction is covered by guaranteed copy elision.',
'Make Message noncopyable and return the prvalue again. Separately study a named local return and distinguish its rule.');

add('templates','deduction','Template argument deduction follows the parameter pattern',
'A function template can infer a type from its arguments. For a by-value parameter, deduction produces a value type suitable for the local parameter, while a reference parameter preserves a borrowing relationship.\n\nDeduction and conversion are separate stages. When arguments imply conflicting template types, an explicit design choice may be needed instead of assuming arithmetic conversions will reconcile them.',
cpp('iostream',"    std::cout << maximum(3, 7) << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << maximum<double>(3, 7.5) << \"\\n\";","template <class T> T maximum(T a, T b) {\n    return a < b ? b : a;\n}"),'7\n7.5',
'The first call deduces int. The second explicitly chooses double so both arguments are accepted in that domain.',
'maximum(3,7.5) does not deduce a single T merely because both arguments are arithmetic.',
['Why does the second call name double explicitly?','To choose one shared parameter type','To enable runtime dispatch','To allocate the result'],'The explicit template argument resolves the intended common domain.',
'Attempt the mixed call without an explicit argument and read the conflicting-deduction diagnostic before designing an overload.');
add('templates','requires-expressions','A requires expression can check an operation',
'A requires expression asks whether a particular expression or declaration is well-formed for a candidate type. A concept packages that structural requirement into a reusable interface constraint.\n\nA syntactic requirement cannot prove all semantic promises. A method named size could still return an incorrect count; tests and documentation carry that additional contract.',
cpp('concepts iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values{1, 2, 3};\n    std::cout << count(values) << \"\\n\";","template <class T>\nconcept Sized = requires(const T &value) {\n    { value.size() } -> std::convertible_to<std::size_t>;\n};\ntemplate <Sized T> std::size_t count(const T &value) {\n    return value.size();\n}"),'3',
'vector satisfies the size-expression requirement and its result can convert to size_t.',
'A concept that checks an expression does not automatically check complexity, lifetime, or thread-safety guarantees.',
['What does Sized establish?','That the required size expression is well-formed','That every size implementation is correct','That size is always constant time'],'The requirement is structural; semantic promises remain separate.',
'Create a small user type satisfying Sized and a type without size. Compare the accepted call with the constraint diagnostic.');
add('templates','if-constexpr','Select a compile-time branch',
'if constexpr discards one branch during template instantiation according to a constant condition. This lets an implementation use operations available only for certain types without requiring the discarded branch to be valid for that instantiation.\n\nOrdinary if chooses at runtime and generally still requires both branch bodies to be well-formed.',
cpp('iostream type_traits','    print_kind(3);\n    print_kind(3.5);',"template <class T> void print_kind(T) {\n    if constexpr (std::is_integral_v<T>)\n        std::cout << \"integral\\n\";\n    else\n        std::cout << \"other\\n\";\n}"),'integral\nother',
'The two calls instantiate different compile-time branches from the same template definition.',
'A runtime variable cannot be used as the condition of if constexpr unless it is a usable constant expression.',
['When is this branch selected?','During template instantiation','At every loop iteration','During linking based on input data'],'The condition depends on a compile-time type trait.',
'Add a branch that calls a method only present on one user type. Contrast an ordinary if with if constexpr.');
add('templates','constexpr-evaluation','Constexpr permits evaluation in constant-expression contexts',
'A constexpr function can participate in compile-time evaluation when its arguments and operations satisfy the constant-expression rules. It can also be called at runtime.\n\nUse static_assert to check a compile-time property, and ordinary validation for runtime inputs. constexpr does not imply that every invocation must execute during compilation.',
cpp('iostream','    static_assert(triangle(4)==10);\n    int input=5;\n    std::cout << triangle(input) << "\\n";','constexpr int triangle(int n) {int total=0;for(int i=1;i<=n;++i)total+=i;return total;}'),'15',
'The assertion requires a compile-time result for four. The ordinary call remains a valid function call for the local input.',
'Compile-time evaluation still has arithmetic and resource limits; overflowing a signed result is not made valid by constexpr.',
['Does constexpr force every call to be compile-time?','No','Yes','Only for int return types'],'It enables constant evaluation where the context requires or permits it.',
'Add static assertions for zero and one. Specify a bounded input domain that keeps the sum representable.');
add('templates','forwarding','Forward a callable without inventing ownership',
'A forwarding reference can preserve an argument’s value category through a generic wrapper. std::forward is used with the deduced template parameter when passing that argument onward.\n\nForwarding is useful at API boundaries, but it does not extend an object’s lifetime. Returning or storing a forwarded reference still needs an ownership and lifetime plan.',
cpp('functional iostream utility',"    int total = 0;\n    auto add = [&](int value) { total += value; };\n    invoke_once(add, 4);\n    invoke_once([](int value) { std::cout << value << \"\\n\"; }, 9);\n    std::cout << total << \"\\n\";","template <class F, class T> void invoke_once(F &&function, T &&value) {\n    std::invoke(std::forward<F>(function), std::forward<T>(value));\n}"),'9\n4',
'The wrapper invokes an lvalue lambda and a temporary lambda without making them persistent owners. Each call completes before its arguments expire.',
'Using std::move unconditionally in a forwarding wrapper can move from a caller’s lvalue unexpectedly.',
['Why use forward with a deduced parameter?','To preserve the caller argument category','To always copy the object','To keep temporaries alive forever'],'The wrapper preserves rather than unconditionally changes the argument category.',
'Wrap a move-only callable and test passing it as an lvalue and as a temporary. Avoid returning a borrowed reference from the wrapper.');

add('polymorphism','virtual-interface','Dispatch through a borrowing base reference',
'A virtual member selects behavior using an object’s dynamic type when called through a base pointer or reference. An interface can express a storage operation without requiring callers to know the implementation type.\n\nThe reference in this example borrows an already owned object. Dynamic dispatch and dynamic allocation are independent choices.',
cpp('iostream',"    MemoryStore store;\n    const Store &interface = store;\n    std::cout << interface.count() << \"\\n\";","struct Store {\n    virtual ~Store() = default;\n    virtual int count() const = 0;\n};\nstruct MemoryStore final : Store {\n    int count() const override {\n        return 3;\n    }\n};"),'3',
'The base reference calls MemoryStore::count. The concrete object remains an automatic value owned by main.',
'A base interface used for polymorphic deletion needs an appropriate virtual destructor.',
['Does virtual dispatch require heap allocation?','No','Yes','Only when the interface has a destructor'],'The dynamic type can belong to an ordinary local object.',
'Add a second implementation and call both through a const Store reference. Keep construction and lifetime ownership explicit.');
add('polymorphism','virtual-destruction','Delete polymorphic objects through a valid owner',
'When a unique_ptr<Base> owns a Derived object, deletion occurs through the base interface. A virtual base destructor ensures the most-derived destructor runs before base cleanup.\n\nThis is a resource-management contract, not merely a dispatch detail. Prefer an explicit virtual destructor on an owning polymorphic base.',
cpp('iostream memory',"    std::unique_ptr<Base> owner = std::make_unique<Derived>();\n    owner.reset();","struct Base {\n    virtual ~Base() {\n        std::cout << \"base\\n\";\n    }\n};\nstruct Derived : Base {\n    ~Derived() override {\n        std::cout << \"derived\\n\";\n    }\n};"),'derived\nbase',
'Resetting the owner first destroys the Derived part, then the Base part. The output exposes the required order.',
'Deleting a derived object through an unsuitable non-virtual base destructor is not repaired by wrapping that base pointer in unique_ptr.',
['Which destructor runs first?','Derived','Base','Only the declared pointer type is destroyed'],'Virtual destruction begins with the most-derived object.',
'Add an owning member to Derived and verify it is released. Explain why resource safety depends on the base deletion contract.');
add('polymorphism','object-slicing','Copying a base value can discard derived state',
'A base object has space only for its base subobject. Initializing a Base value from a Derived therefore copies the base portion and slices away the derived portion.\n\nUse references or an explicit owning polymorphic representation when behavior must remain dynamic. Value-based alternatives such as variant can preserve a closed set of complete types.',
cpp('iostream',"    Derived derived;\n    Base sliced = derived;\n    const Base &borrowed = derived;\n    std::cout << sliced.kind() << \" \" << borrowed.kind() << \"\\n\";","struct Base {\n    virtual ~Base() = default;\n    virtual int kind() const {\n        return 1;\n    }\n};\nstruct Derived : Base {\n    int kind() const override {\n        return 2;\n    }\n};"),'1 2',
'The sliced value is a complete Base, so it reports one. The reference still refers to the original Derived and reports two.',
'A vector<Base> does not preserve arbitrary derived objects merely because Base has virtual functions.',
['What is sliced’s dynamic type?','Base','Derived','A combination chosen at each call'],'It is a newly constructed Base value containing only the base portion.',
'Compare vector<Base>, vector<unique_ptr<Base>>, and vector<variant<A,B>> for ownership, extensibility, and copying.');
add('polymorphism','variant-visitation','Visit a closed set of complete value types',
'variant owns one active alternative from a fixed set. A visitor handles the active type without a manually synchronized integer tag and pointer. This fits domains where the alternatives are known and value semantics are useful.\n\nA variant can become valueless in some exceptional assignments, so do not generalize one simple fixture into an unconditional no-failure claim.',
cpp('iostream variant',"    std::variant<Scan, Lookup> plan = Lookup{7};\n    std::visit([](const auto &operation) { operation.explain(); }, plan);","struct Scan {\n    void explain() const {\n        std::cout << \"scan\\n\";\n    }\n};\nstruct Lookup {\n    int id;\n    void explain() const {\n        std::cout << \"lookup \" << id << \"\\n\";\n    }\n};"),'lookup 7',
'The variant owns a Lookup value. The generic visitor is instantiated for both alternatives and invokes the active one.',
'Adding a new alternative requires checking every visitor that depends on exhaustive handling.',
['Does this variant store a borrowed Lookup pointer?','No, it owns the active value','Yes','Only when the alternative is small'],'The complete Lookup object is stored as the active alternative.',
'Add a RangeLookup alternative and extend the explanation contract. Compare compiler diagnostics from an incomplete visitor.');
add('polymorphism','composition','Compose behavior before adding inheritance',
'Composition makes a component an owned member or an explicitly borrowed collaborator. It is often sufficient when the relationship is “uses a policy” rather than “is a substitutable implementation.”\n\nA small policy value can make a rule testable without virtual dispatch or shared lifetime ownership. Keep the policy’s state and mutation contract visible.',
cpp('iostream',"    Store store{ScorePolicy{100}};\n    std::cout << store.accepts(90) << \" \" << store.accepts(101) << \"\\n\";","struct ScorePolicy {\n    unsigned maximum;\n    bool accepts(unsigned score) const {\n        return score <= maximum;\n    }\n};\nstruct Store {\n    ScorePolicy policy;\n    bool accepts(unsigned score) const {\n        return policy.accepts(score);\n    }\n};"),'1 0',
'Store delegates the range decision to its policy member. Both values remain ordinary owned objects.',
'Inheritance used solely for code reuse can expose an unintended substitutability contract.',
['What relationship is represented here?','The store owns and uses a policy value','Store is a kind of score','The policy shares ownership of every row'],'The policy is a component, not a base-class identity.',
'Add a minimum score to the policy and test its boundaries independently before integrating it into the store.');
}
